          </div>
          <p>Here are the top 10 most confused word pairings, ordered from most to least common.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cw-top10-grid">
          {
            top10.map((item, index) => (
              <a
                class:list={["cw-top-card", `cw-accent-${(index % 6) + 1}`, index < 3 && "cw-top-card--featured"]}
                href={item.href}
              >
                <span class="cw-rank">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                <strong>{item.label}</strong>
                <b>↗</b>
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-explainer">
      <div class="cw-shell">
        <div class="cw-explainer__grid">
          <article class="cw-explainer-card cw-explainer-card--dark">
            <span class="cw-mini-label cw-mini-label--lime">HOMONYMS, HOMOPHONES & HOMOGRAPHS</span>
            <h2>They can sound the same.<br />They can look the same.</h2>
            <p>
              Some of the easily confused pairs are <a href="https://www.grammar-monster.com/glossary/homonyms.htm">homonyms</a>.
              These are words that either sound exactly the same or look exactly the same.
            </p>
            <p>
              Homonyms that sound the same (e.g., <strong>bare</strong>/<strong>bear</strong>)
              are called <em>homophones</em>, and homonyms that look the same
              (e.g., <strong>lead</strong> of a pencil/<strong>lead</strong> a team)
              are called <em>homographs</em>. In writing, homophones are the ones that cause problems.
            </p>
            <p>
              Most of the easily confused pairs of words are <em>near homonyms</em>.
              In other words, they are pairs that nearly sound or look the same.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="cw-explainer-card cw-errors">
            <span class="cw-mini-label">SPELLING ERRORS UNDERMINE YOUR WORK</span>
            <h2>A spellchecker cannot save you from everything.</h2>
            <p>
              Choosing the wrong one in a pair of easily confused words is usually classified
              as a spelling error rather than a grammatical error, but you cannot rely on your
              spellchecker – or your grammar checker for that matter – to spot your error because
              there's a fair chance the error will be a feasibly sound piece of English text.
            </p>

            <div class="cw-example-pairs">
              <div>
                <span>He had <strong>bare</strong> hands.</span>
                <b>✓</b>
                <small>no gloves</small>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>He had <strong>bear</strong> hands.</span>
                <b>✓</b>
                <small>hands like a bear</small>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>He went <strong>forth</strong>.</span>
                <b>✓</b>
                <small>forward</small>
              </div>
              <div>
                <span>He went <strong>fourth</strong>.</span>
                <b>✓</b>
                <small>one after third</small>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p class="cw-error-note">
              Any errors in your work – especially <a href="https://www.grammar-monster.com/common_grammar_errors.htm">common ones</a> –
              will undermine your credibility, and this can prevent your readers from accepting your message.
              Put another way, your readers are likely to concentrate on your mistakes rather than what you're saying.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-glossary" id="glossary">
      <div class="cw-shell">
        <div class="cw-glossary__head">
          <div>
            <span class="cw-mini-label">A–Z GLOSSARY</span>
            <h2>220 easily confused<br /><em>word pairings.</em></h2>
          </div>
          <p>
            Here is our A-Z list of 220 easily confused word pairs.
            Every link leads to a lesson with an interactive exercise, which is printable and sendable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cw-search-panel">
          <div class="cw-searchbox">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="m21 21-4.2-4.2m1.2-5.8A7 7 0 1 1 4 11a7 7 0 0 1 14 0Z"></path>
            </svg>
            <input
              id="confusedSearch"
              type="search"
              placeholder="Search 220 pairings — try ‘affect’, ‘there’, ‘principal’..."
              autocomplete="off"
            />
            <kbd>/</kbd>
          </div>

          <div class="cw-search-meta">
            <span id="confusedCount">220 pairs</span>
            <button id="clearConfused" type="button">Clear search</button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <nav class="cw-alphabet" aria-label="Jump to a letter">
          {
            letters.map((group) => (
              <button
                type="button"
                data-letter={group.letter}
                disabled={group.entries.length === 0}
                title={group.entries.length === 0 ? `No ${group.letter} entries` : `Jump to ${group.letter}`}
              >
                {group.letter}
              </button>
            ))
          }
        </nav>

        <div class="cw-letter-list">
          {
            letters.map((group, groupIndex) => (
              <section
                class:list={["cw-letter-section", `cw-accent-${(groupIndex % 6) + 1}`]}
                data-pair-group
                data-letter-section={group.letter}
                hidden={group.entries.length === 0}
              >
                <div class="cw-letter-rail">
                  <strong>{group.letter}</strong>
                  <span>{group.entries.length} {group.entries.length === 1 ? "pair" : "pairs"}</span>
                </div>

                <div class="cw-pair-grid">
                  {
                    group.entries.map((entry) => (
                      <div class="cw-pair" data-pair data-search={entry.label.toLowerCase()}>
                        <a class="cw-pair__main" href={entry.href}>
                          <span>{entry.label}</span>
                          <b>↗</b>
                        </a>

                        {
                          entry.video && (
                            <a
                              class="cw-pair__video"
                              href={entry.video}
                              target="_blank"
                              rel="noopener"
                              title="Video tutorial"
                              aria-label={`Watch a video about ${entry.label}`}
                            >
                              ▶
                            </a>
                          )
                        }
                      </div>
                    ))
                  }
                </div>
              </section>
            ))
          }
        </div>

        <div class="cw-empty" id="confusedEmpty" hidden>
          <span>?</span>
          <h3>No matching word pairing found.</h3>
          <p>Try a broader search or use the A–Z picker above.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-teacher">
      <div class="cw-shell cw-teacher__grid">
        <div class="cw-teacher__art">
          <div class="cw-genius-disc">
            <img src="/assets/images/genius.png" alt="Grammar Monster genius character" />
          </div>
          <div class="cw-genius-ring"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="cw-teacher__copy">
          <span class="cw-mini-label cw-mini-label--lime">TEACHERS' ZONE</span>
          <h2>Teach it.<br />Test it.<br /><em>Make it stick.</em></h2>
          <p>
            Use Grammar Monster's existing games, confidence tests and reference pages
            to practise easily confused words in class or at home.
          </p>

          <div class="cw-teacher-links">
            {
              teacherZone.map((item, index) => (
                <a href={item.href}>
                  <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                  <div>
                    <small>{item.type}</small>
                    <strong>{item.label}</strong>
                  </div>
                  <b>↗</b>
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cw-related">
      <div class="cw-shell">
        <div class="cw-section-head">
          <div>
            <span class="cw-mini-label">KEEP EXPLORING</span>
            <h2>You might also like…</h2>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cw-related-grid">
          {
            related.map((item, index) => (
              <a class:list={[`cw-accent-${(index % 6) + 1}`]} href={item.href}>
                <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
                <strong>{item.label}</strong>
                <b>↗</b>
              </a>
            ))
          }
        </div>

        <div class="cw-author">
          <div class="cw-author__mark">✎</div>
          <div>
            <small>ORIGINAL GRAMMAR MONSTER CONTENT</small>
            <p>
              This page was written by
              <a href="https://www.grammar-monster.com/Craig_Shrives_author.htm">Craig Shrives</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script>
    const search = document.getElementById("confusedSearch");
    const groups = [...document.querySelectorAll("[data-pair-group]")];
    const count = document.getElementById("confusedCount");
    const empty = document.getElementById("confusedEmpty");

    function filterPairs(value) {
      const term = (value || "").trim().toLowerCase();
      let visible = 0;

      groups.forEach((group) => {
        let groupVisible = 0;

        group.querySelectorAll("[data-pair]").forEach((item) => {
          const match = !term || (item.dataset.search || "").includes(term);
          item.hidden = !match;

          if (match) {
            visible++;
            groupVisible++;
          }
        });

        group.hidden = groupVisible === 0;
      });

      if (count) count.textContent = `${visible} ${visible === 1 ? "pair" : "pairs"}`;
      if (empty) empty.hidden = visible !== 0;
    }

    search?.addEventListener("input", () => filterPairs(search.value));

    document.getElementById("clearConfused")?.addEventListener("click", () => {
      if (search) {
        search.value = "";
        search.focus();
      }
      filterPairs("");
    });

    document.querySelectorAll("[data-letter]").forEach((button) => {
      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const target = document.querySelector(`[data-letter-section="${button.dataset.letter}"]`);

        if (search) search.value = "";
        filterPairs("");
        target?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "start" });
      });
    });

    document.addEventListener("keydown", (event) => {
      const tag = document.activeElement?.tagName;

      if (event.key === "/" && tag !== "INPUT" && tag !== "TEXTAREA") {
        event.preventDefault();
        search?.focus();
      }

      if (event.key === "Escape" && document.activeElement === search) {
        search.value = "";
        filterPairs("");
        search.blur();
      }
    });
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
